 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 233667,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Eukaryotic mitochondria contain their own DNA, known as mitochondrial DNA, or mtDNA. Nuclear and mitochondrial DNA are thought to be of different (separate) evolutionary origin, with the mitochondrial DNA being derived from the circular chromosomes of bacteria that were engulfed by ancient prokaryotic cells. Does the presence of mitochondrial DNA suggest that organisms share many conserved core processes and features that evolved and are widely distributed among organisms today? Why or why not?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 724816,
     "content_html": "Yes, because it shows the prokaryotes and eukaryotes use similar organelles, namely, mitochondria."
    },
    {
     "id": 724817,
     "content_html": "Yes, because it suggests the eukaryotes possess traits that were likely conserved from prokaryotic ancestors."
    },
    {
     "id": 724815,
     "content_html": "No, because mitochondrial DNA is very different from the DNA within a eukaryote’s nucleus."
    },
    {
     "id": 724814,
     "content_html": "No, because mitochondrial DNA is not used by the eukaryotic cells."
    }
   ],
